[Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  in Wolfenstein alternate 1980s Nazis  remain a force of evil across Europe  thus the Nazi killing continues as a  blaskowitz twins Justin so pick up where  their parents left off for this spinoff  Wolfenstein Youngblood it's a relentless  co-op RPG light shooter driven by an  unapologetic youthful attitude it may  not reach the same heights of its  predecessors but Youngblood hits where  it counts Justin self and their best  friend Abbi immediately come to life  with a striking contrast from their  parents their boisterous and sometimes  dorky the same way many teenagers and  young adults are it gives them genuine  personalities that mostly come off cool  as hell especially with stellar voice  acting and silly moments like elevator  loading screens with a decent pop tunes  [Music]  with BJ gone missing they uncover clues  to his disappearance and take matters  into their own hands  there were out Nazi occupied France to  find their dad and well kill Nazis as  either Jess or Soph  with their co-op or AI partner as the  other sister you join a French  resistance movement in new Paris which  quickly boils down to you raiding Nazi  outposts and strongholds with them slick  dot you shoot suits with the twins  inseparable  co-op is at the heart of the experience  and thankfully partnering up online is a  breeze you can have friends or randoms  jump into your session seamlessly  without interruption the AI will assume  control until a player connects and  again right as a player leaves and when  players have identical missions in their  quest log completing it records progress  for both players  and if you rather go it alone alongside  a decent AI companion it's just as  viable for the entire game  sláinte gameplay-wise  Youngblood captures that familiar  Wolfenstein feeling of taking an  automatic shotgun to a Nazi soldier  melting an armor clad super soldier with  a laser rifle or zapping a horde with a  lightning coil and what a powerful  feeling it is what's new is a number of  RPG light twists tougher enemies have  one of two armor elements that are weak  to corresponding weapons encouraging you  to actively juggle your varied arsenal a  slightly more diverse weapon upgrade  system helps flesh out some familiar  firearms there's also a character  progression system where you rack up XP  to level up then dump upgrade points  into new skills and perks  these are simple systems that help  facilitate steady unlocks making you  feel like you're getting ever more  devastating but never overpowered solid  gunplay and some neat mechanics wouldn't  mean much without the proper combat  encounters to complement them and  Youngblood delivers you'll often pull  out all the stops to either finish  combat scenarios or realize that you  have to retreat to rethink your approach  stealth alone isn't really an option but  it's a good way to thin out the  opposition before going all-out guns  blazing it can get overwhelming when  super soldiers massive mechs and a bomb  strapped panzer who and bear down on you  but that's when Youngblood is at its  best intense firefights can break out  anywhere with little warning and the  main missions manage to keep a  consistent action-packed momentum  throughout  Youngblood leans more into an open  structure about making new Paris a group  of separate districts where you find  your missions the main questline  branches from the hub areas but out on  the streets side missions and random  events fill in the spaces listen up  sisters there's a high-value target  using a car parked near your location  get there and plant a bomb on it  good luck sisters there conducive for  racking up early XP getting familiar  with district layouts and soaking up the  vibe of a downtrodden 1980s pears but  quickly feel like filler that bulk out  your to-do list levels do have a hint of  Arkane Studios influence sometimes  reminding me of dishonored it spices up  combat with some verticality and allows  light exploration for alternate routes  or collectables it's a successful  incorporation of that studio strengths  and the game is better for it  now the Paris catacombs act as your safe  hub in Youngblood it's not as extensive  as the new Colossus is u-boat home and  you won't get much from the inhabitants  as there are nowhere near as involved as  Wolfenstein 2 supporting cast they're  more just quest givers  however just tsofen abbey are there to  pick up the slack i decrypted fragments  of stolen Nazi Intel on the computer  witness reports from Nazis who survived  the encounter with Captain blaskowitz  piecing together the fragmented  information I deduced that he was  looking for a place called lab X where  he must be been the story overall  doesn't reach the same highs as the new  Colossus  it's an incredibly tough act to follow  really but aside from a cheap plot twist  and underwhelming villains most of young  bloods lean story is a fun ride to that  end the game is less about a bold  fleshed out narrative and more about  instilling an infectious charisma in its  star characters to match the  over-the-top action and it sews the  seats for what's next in Wolfenstein  leaving the door wide open for some  exciting wild possibilities there are  traces of an ongoing game here you can  take on daily and weekly challenges as  they cycle into the game or replay story  missions on harder difficulties for  increasing amounts of XP and currency  which is a bit more enticing while it's  a bog-standard way to keep the co-op  experience going they at least offer an  outlet to try new tactics as these  harder modes can be quite unforgiving  the end game may not be extensive but  the ride was exciting enough that the  content feels like a little value-added  mr. Wolfenstein Youngblood has a series  signature first person shooting thrills  that will have you gladly busting shots  and blasting lasers in the face of Nazi  trash and the opportunity to do so  alongside a friend the fight carries on  to the next generation and the way the  spin-off bronze the saga with the  blaskowitz twins makes you wish there  was more to see from this new cast of  lovable knuckleheads Jess and self and  Abby to learn from the best and they  embrace their newfound duty of ridding  the world of tyranny while being cool as  hell doing it Youngblood is short but oh  so sweet stewardesses just like daddy  told us  [Music]

